    "passage": "The baobab tree is a prehistoric species that predates the existence of mankind and the splitting of the continents. Its magnificent image is an icon of the African continent and [[1]] many traditional remedies and folklore. Living in the harsh and dry climate of the African savannah, it stands for life and strength in a landscape that is [[2]] to most living things. Due to centuries of evolution, the baobab [[3]] to store water in its large trunk during rainy periods. This enables it to bear nutrient-dense fruit in the dry seasons, earning it the title “The Tree of Life.”\nUnique among fruits, the baobab fruit naturally dries while still attached to the tree. After over six months under the sun on its branches, its green, silky exterior will [[4]] a tough coconut-like shell, protecting the dried pulp inside. This process allows the fruit to be easily harvested, deseeded, and processed into a fine powder without spoiling. Remarkably, every part of the baobab tree [[5]]: its bark can be fashioned into ropes and clothing, its seeds produced into oils for skincare, its leaves consumed as food, its trunk utilized for water storage, and its fruit, packed with nutrients and antioxidants, has been a source of African women’s health and beauty for generations.",
